    "h1": "HTS Chapter 29: Organic Chemicals — U.S. Exports & Foreign Tariffs",
    "seo": {
      "title": "U.S. Organic Chemicals Exports and Foreign Tariffs (HTS Ch. 29) | KoalaGains",
      "shortDescription": "U.S. organic chemical exports were $59.18B in 2025, up 14.1%. Where they go, what the EU, China and Mexico charge, and which tariffs changed since January 2025.",
      "keywords": ["US chemical exports", "US organic chemicals exports", "tariffs on US chemicals", "China tariff on US chemicals", "HS 29 exports by country"]
    },
    "lastCheckedAt": "2026-10-20"
  },
  "intro": "The U.S. exported **$59.18B** of organic chemicals in 2025, up 14.1% on 2024 and 74% above 2020 ($33.97B). One heading drove the jump: hormones and steroids (2937), which include insulin and other peptide hormones, were **32%** of the total ($19.09B, up 91.6%). Italy alone bought $16.04B (up 128.5%) and the EU as a whole $28.94B, 49% of the chapter. The rest is bulk intermediates — acyclic hydrocarbons (2901, $9.30B), alcohols (2905) and ethers (2909) — sold to Mexico, Canada, China and Japan.\n\nTariffs are low or zero for most buyers: the EU cut its duties to zero on July 1, 2026 (except mannitol and sorbitol), and Mexico, Canada and South Korea charge nothing on qualifying goods. **China** is the exception: it adds a 10% extra duty to its MFN rates and bought 13.3% less. What usually decides access is chemical registration (REACH, K-REACH, KKDIK, China's Order No. 12) and, for drug ingredients, the medicines regulator — plus U.S. rules such as TSCA export notices and DEA and Chemical Weapons Convention controls.",
  "latestYear": 2025,
  "priorYear": 2024,
  "valueBasis": "FOB value of U.S. exports, as reported by the United States to UN Comtrade.",
